NAME
    sed - stream editor (substitution only)

SYNOPSIS
    sed s/regex/replacement/[g] [file ...]

DESCRIPTION
    Applies one substitution to each line of the files named (read as
    one stream; globs expand, "-" is the console), or of the console up
    to Ctrl-D, and prints the result. Only the s command exists. The
    left side is a basic regex (. * + ? ^ $, see regex); the
    replacement is literal text. Without g only the first match of each
    line is replaced, with g (or G) every one. sed is /BIN/SED.

    The match replaced is the shortest one: * and + are not greedy, so
    s/a.*e/X/ stops at the first e after the a. An empty match is
    skipped, so s/x*/Y/ changes nothing. The s command runs from s/ to
    its third /, so the regex and the replacement may hold spaces but
    not a /.

    Errors: "sed: only s/re/new/[g]", "sed: bad s/// (no second /)".

LIMITS
    The regex and the replacement are cut at 63 characters; input and
    output lines at 255.

EXAMPLES
    sed s/red/RED/ /FRUIT.TXT      the first "red" of each line
    sed s/an+/AN/g /FRUIT.TXT      banana becomes bANANa
    sed s/^.*e/X/ /FRUIT2.TXT      apple 3 red becomes X 3 red
    sed s/ 12 / twelve / /FRUIT.TXT
                                   spaces in the s command

NOTES
    Each file ends a line.
    Removed from the P8X version: input from a pipe or a < redirect.
    New: several files, globs and "-".
    Ported from P8X os/commands/sed.c 2026-09-23.

SEE ALSO
